\chapter{Conclusion}
  \label{chap:conclusion}
  The objective of this study was to examine the subject pronoun systems of Creoles and Cajuns and how these systems relate to and construct speakers’ identities.
  In pursuing that objective, three research questions were asked.
  The first asked whether general tendencies can be observed between Creole and Cajun participants' subject pronoun usage.
  For 1sg, 2sg.T, 3sg.F, 3sg.M, 1pl, 3pl, and impersonal subject pronouns, Creoles were found to prefer \lexi{mo}, \lexi{to}, \lexi{li}, \lexi{li}, \lexi{no}, \lexi{yé}, and \lexi{to}, respectively, whereas Cajauns were found to prefer \lexi{je}, \lexi{tu}, \lexi{elle}, \lexi{il}, \lexi{on}, \lexi{ils/ça}, and \lexi{tu}, constituting what have been called here Creole-marked and Cajun-marked pronouns.
  This provided the linguistic context that speakers then navigated.
  The second question asked what impact ethnic homophily in personal networks has on subject pronoun usage.
  There was in fact no quantitatively observable relationship between the degree of ethnic homophily and the degree to which Creole participants used Cajun-marked subject pronouns or Cajun participanats used Creole-marked subject pronouns.
  It was notable, however, Creoles generally did not name Cajun alters and Cajuns did not name Creole alters when asked with whom they discuss important matters and with whom they speak F\&C.
  Finally, the third question asked how Creole and Cajuns constructed social meanings and their identities relative to these patterns.
  Their discourse suggested that, for these speakers, Creole-marked subject pronouns index Blackness but also closeness to Louisiana, whereas Cajun-marked subject pronouns indexed various types of formality and distance.
  What these general results point to is that F\&C subject pronouns are tied up in ethnic identities but are also malleable in terms of the work they can do in meaning-making and identity construction.

  % The objective of this study was to examine F\&C-speaking Creoles and Cajuns in Louisiana for signs of their racialization.
  % One research question did so by asking whether subject pronouns vary according to these identities, and they did, suggesting that Creoles and Cajuns can be identified via their speech.
  % This identification facilitates racialization.
  % A second research question asked whether subject pronouns also vary according to how ethnically homophilous Creoles' and Cajuns' personal networks are, and they did not.
  % However, this was likely because their personal networks were almost all highly homophilous, which is expected with racialized groups.
  % Finally, a third research question asked if Creoles and Cajuns mark, police, and maintain boundaries around their identities in ways that are consistent with racialized groups, and they did.
  % The overall picture presented by this study therefore suggests that Creoles and Cajuns are in fact racialized.

  In the broad quantitative analyses of Chapter \ref{chap:pronoun_variation}, one subject pronoun system was found to be associated with Creoles and other with Cajuns.
  While important, these were not the only associations that were found.
  The following verb type was a meaningful structural constraint for those speakers who used \lexi{ils} and \lexi{ça} for 3pl, where the verbs \lexi{être} \gloss{to be} and \lexi{avoir} \gloss{to have} led to greater use of the former and all other verb types led to greater use of the latter.
  Some aspects of this factor had been found previously in the literature \parencite{brown_pronominal_1988, byers_defining_1988}, but the results here more clearly narrowed in on exactly which verbs are important for subject pronouns.
  
  Exposure to institutional French had a much more consistent effect across subject pronoun types.
  In short, exposure to institutional French made participants more likely to produce Cajun-marked pronouns.
  This may very well have been related to the greater resemblance between those pronouns taught in French classrooms and Cajun-marked pronouns than between classroom pronouns and Creole-marked pronouns where what was heard in the classroom either reinforced the valuation of what speakers already used when entering the classroom or otherwise influenced them to shift their subject pronouns.
  Imbedded in this result was something of an effect for socioeconomic class as going to college was itself associated with exposure to institutional French, making this factor a proxy for socioeconomic class.
  A related outcome of this was participants using Cajun-marked pronouns in formal settings, to discuss educational topics, and even to lightly mock individuals whose ideas were seen as incorrect.

  Although it was not part of the statistical models that were fit here, racialized identities also appeared to be associated with certain subject pronouns, perhaps even more so than Creole and Cajun identities.
  Black speakers appeared to have a preference for Creole-marked pronouns and White speakers for Cajun-marked pronouns.
  As there was a statistically significant association between Creole and Cajun identities and identifying as Black or White, it is not surprising that these latter categories seemed to follow the former in their relationships with subject pronouns.
  However, to the extent that one can speak of effect sizes, it is notable that they would not be identical for the two sets of categories.
  Conceptualizations of the local Louisiana categories have quite likely been influenced by broader US racialized categories through the process of fractal recursivity \parencite{irvine_language_2000}, but they have not become identical.
  There remain idiosyncracies to how Louisianians understand Creoles and Cajuns that resist reducing them to simply Black and White, and this appears to play out in how subject pronouns are realized in F\&C.

  % In other words, Creoles and Cajuns have not exactly been racialized on their own terms but by being made to fit into the broader system as the state has become less isolated.
  % This phenomenon is not exactly a new finding in the sociological literature, especially for more marginalized groups such as Creoles, as there have been a number of examinations of how groups become Black, so to speak, when entering the US system \parencite{ducros_youre_2021, grosfoguel_race_2004, ifatunji_toward_2024}.
  % However, this finding had not been established for Creoles and Cajuns even though the idea had been commonly repeated in the literature and even among residents in the form of describing Creoles as Black.
  % What is perhaps more novel is that examinations of the incorporation of groups into the racialized social system of the US have typically involved immigrants, or at least intra-state migrants, rather than long-existing local ethnicities such as Creoles in Louisiana.
  % In a sense, despite Louisiana's status as a US state for over 200 years, it has resembled a foreign territory relative to its neighboring states that the \foreign{Américains}, as effective foreigners to the state, are still colonizing.
  % Additionally novel is the fact that this study did not focus purely on the racialization of the marginalized group but also of the higher status group in Cajuns who have been aligned with Whiteness.
  % Accounting for such groups in a racialized social system is not completely new to sociology, either, although Whiteness has been less studied than the marginalized \parencite[\foreign{cf}.][]{bell_invisible_2021, brodkin_how_1998, carter_racialization_2012, ignatiev_how_1994, lewis_what_2004}.
  % This study has thus injected Cajuns into the discussion on how Whiteness is constructed and expressed.

  A factor that did not prove to be related to subject pronoun variation in F\&C was network ethnic homophily.
  No effect was found that would suggest that, for instance, a Creole whose personal network was made up of fewer Creoles than another Creole's personal network was would use fewer Creole-marked pronouns.
  This was perhaps not surprising given the lack of mixed Creole-Cajun personal networks among the participants.
  Ethnic homophily was generally high, with Cajun participants having mostly Cajuns in their networks and Creole participants mostly Creoles.
  Where homophily was not high, it is not because Creole participants had core or non-core (\foreign{i.e.}, F\&C-related) ties with Cajuns or \foreign{vice versa} but rather that non-Creoles and non-Cajuns made up these other ties.

  In looking at individual speakers who did not conform to the general tendencies that were uncovered for the participants as a whole, network homophily again did not provide any potential explanations for their linguistic behavior.
  Terry, for example, was a Creole partcipiant whose personal network was completely homophilous, yet he did not use a single Creole-marked subject pronoun during his interview.
  Likewise, Elizabeth's network was made up mostly of Cajuns, and while she gave some indications that she is capable of using Cajun-marked pronouns, she only produced three such tokens during the interview, instead using Creole-marked pronouns almost exclusively.
  What perhaps provided better clues about these speakers' linguistic behaviors was place.
  Terry identified as a Creole but spent his life in the towns of Mallet and Swords where Louisiana Creole has not be formally attested, whereas Elizabeth has spent her life in Henderson, which is commonly cited as a town where Creole is spoken.

  The Cajun-marked subject pronouns that Terry used were not only indexical of Cajunness, then.
  Participants indeed used subject pronouns in a variety of ways to enact social meanings and construct their identities.
  Cajun-marked subject pronouns were perhaps indexical of formality, schooling, and distance in addition to the indexicalities that their typical associations with Cajuns and White speakers would give them.
  In terms of formality, some speakers who primarily used Creole-marked pronouns such as Elizabeth and Errol began their interviews using Cajun-marked pronoun, possibly expecting a formal setting or expecting their language to be evaluated on some level.
  In these cases, the association of Cajun-marked pronouns with exposure to institutional French would likewise suggest that speakers see them as more formal.
  Others made very momentary uses of Cajun-marked pronouns in the middle of interviews to emphasize older time periods, such as Floyd, and still others used them during longer lapses to discuss professional settings and disagreements with distant groups of people, such as Owen.

  Creole-marked subject pronouns, alternatively, appeared to be indexical of a sort of closeness or localness.
  Most instances of those who primarily used Cajun-marked pronouns producing Creole-marked pronouns were in fact imitations of the speech of others, be they of Cajun participants imitating Creoles or Creole participants imitating Creole speakers from other regions.
  Otherwise, they were used in situations where the speaker was attempting to establish their credentials as being truly of Louisiana.
  Oliver L. was the clearest case as he volunteered his ability to speak with Creole-marked subject pronouns very early in his interview but otherwise chose to use Cajun-marked pronouns.
  What Oliver L. had in common with others though, such as Errol who regularly used Creole-marked pronouns, is that these were all local small business owners.
  They were able to speak with Creole-marked pronouns because they grew up in towns where Creole was spoken, but they were all capable of speaking with Cajun-marked pronouns yet chose either to demonstrate the ability to switch or chose to speak almost entirely with Creole-marked pronouns.
  Using Creole-marked pronouns possibly gave them a sort of local cachet that would be important for their professional identities that were closely tied to Louisiana.

  These results most directly expand on what has already been found for subject pronouns in F\&C, in part because of the way the linguistic unit of analysis was delineated.
  Previous variationist and documentary work in Louisiana has started with choosing either French or Creole as the unit of analysis, distinguishing between the two with a diagnostic based partially on subject pronouns \parencite{klingler_language_2003, wendte_chronotopic_2022}.
  Studies therefore conceptualized as examining Creole have only included participants who primarily use the 1sg subject pronoun \lexi{mo} rather than \lexi{je}.
  The identities of the speakers in these studies have furthermore most often been categorized in terms of the broad racialized US categories of Black and White.
  Cajuns have found little place in these works, mostly being relegated to brief mentions that White Louisianians often identify as Cajuns before moving back to the Black versus White framing \parencite[\foreign{e..g.},][p.~xxvii]{klingler_if_2003}.
  While this has provided useful data on the relationship between racialized US categories and linguistic features produced by F\&C speakers who prefer \lexi{mo} for 1sg, it has necessarily left out starker variation in subject pronouns as well as analyses of speakers who are explicitly self-identified Cajuns, leaving one to wonder how many of the White speakers in studies of Creole are in fact Cajuns such as Errol and Elizabeth.

  On the other hand, studied conceptualized as examining French have only included participants who primarily use the 1sg subject \lexi{je}.
  Cajuns are quite central to these studies, although they have at times included members of indigenous tribes such as the Houma in southeast Louisiana \parencite{dajko_ethnic_2009, rottet_language_1995}.
  Creoles such as Terry, James, Samantha, Jerry, and Deborah are also \lexi{je} users, though.
  Not only are Creoles not present in studies of French in Louisiana, the fact that their racialized identities can vary more than Cajuns' has meant that various possible intersectional identities have not been analyzed.
  White Creoles such as Jerry and Deborah, for instance, patterned here with Cajuns rather than with Black Creoles, demonstrating that racialized identities can sometimes trump local ethnic categories.
  Larger samples of of speakers would be needed to confirm this pattern, however.

  The present study, then, is not only the first to take a more open approach to which speakers could participate but is also the first to include both Cajuns and Creoles outside of examinations of language ideologies \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][]{klingler_language_2003}
  This has allowed for a greater understanding of the range of pronoun systems that F\&C speakers employ by being able to capture those Cajuns and Creoles who do not fall more neatly into broader patterns.
  It has also allowed for a greater understanding of how Cajuns and Creoles navigate these systems, make social meaning out of them, and construct their identities.

  In part, the results here have actually validated the above choices that researchers have made.
  Researchers have taken \lexi{mo} to identify their unit of analysis as Creole, and Creoles, who when pushed call their language Creole, primarily produced \lexi{mo} in the present study.
  Likewise, researchers have taken \lexi{je} to identify their unit of analysis as French, and Cajuns, who when pushed call their language Cajun (French), primarily produced \lexi{je} in the present study.
  While these pieces fit rather neatly together, this study has also highlighted that this is certainly not the whole picture.
  Perhaps just as important as the role of local ethnic categories is the role of place in that regional variation may help explain why some speakers do not fit into the picture just described.
  Participants in the present study also showed evidence of an understanding of regional variation in subject pronouns that sometimes superceded the categories of Creole and Cajun.
  It is possible that one could speak of \lexi{mo} regions and \lexi{je} regions or perhaps of \lexi{mo-to-li-no-yé} regions, \lexi{je-tu-il-elle-on-ils-ça} regions, \lexi{je-tu-il-elle-on-eux regions}, and so on.
  Indeed, in Terrebonne-Lafourche in the southeast of Louisiana, both Cajuns and Indians were found to generally agree on their preferred subject pronouns and only differ in their rates of usage of dispreferred pronouns \parencite{dajko_ethnic_2009, rottet_language_1995}, but their preferences matched neither the patterns of Cajuns in the present study nor those of Creoles.
  Something of a call for greater focus on regional variation was was in fact made quite some time ago \parencite{baronian_pre-acadian_2005}, but perhaps this call needs to be reaffirmed, especially in light of more general findings in sociolinguistics that has shown the importance of speakers' orientations to place for their speech patterns \parencite{bridwell_interactions_2026, carmichael_displacement_2017}.

